WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.300
Use your records: real profit, safe loans, and phone records.

2
00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:08.910
This is the video for Module 4, Using records to grow your business.

3
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:15.300
Writing records every day is the hard part. Reading them is the rewarding part.

4
00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:27.220
Once a month, ask five questions. Are sales going up or down? Which products make the most money? Is stock going missing? Are debts growing? And is there enough cash to restock?

5
00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:36.150
For each main product you need two numbers: what you pay for it, and what you sell it for. The difference is your profit per item.

6
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:44.390
Do not try to fix everything at once. Choose the single most important finding and one clear action with a date.

7
00:00:45.640 --> 00:01:03.740
For example, Ifeoma sells household goods in Onitsha. A detergent pack costs her about 1,500 naira and sells for 1,800. That is only 300 naira profit. But she sells about 60 a week, so it earns 18,000 naira.

8
00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:15.400
A bowl set costs 4,000 naira and sells for 6,000, a profit of 2,000 naira. But she sells only about 5 a week, so it earns 10,000.

9
00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:23.340
A fast seller with a small profit can earn more than a slow seller with a big one. So she keeps detergent always in stock.

10
00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:37.990
If you buy goods but sell only part of them, the unsold goods are still on your shelf. So first work out the cost of only what actually left the shop. Opening stock, plus purchases, minus closing stock.

11
00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:42.970
Then gross profit is sales minus that cost of goods sold.

12
00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:52.840
Then net profit is gross profit minus running costs, like transport, levy, packaging, phone, staff, charges and bad debts.

13
00:01:53.190 --> 00:02:00.740
Value stock at what you paid, not your selling price. And do not skip the stock count, or your profit can be badly wrong.

14
00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:16.900
For example, Halima runs a provisions stall. Her opening stock was worth about 150,000 naira at cost. She bought 520,000 more, and at month end her closing stock was 170,000.

15
00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:23.980
Her sales for October, including credit sales, were about 744,000 naira.

16
00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:39.310
Her cost of goods sold is 150,000 plus 520,000 minus 170,000. That is 500,000 naira. So her gross profit is 244,000.

17
00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.080
Her running costs, including one bad debt, were 78,000 naira.

18
00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:57.230
So her net profit is about 166,000 naira. She drew 80,000 for the household, so about 86,000 stayed in the business as extra capital.

19
00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:10.110
Lenders want to see that your business is real, steady and able to pay. Your records answer that. But check for yourself first. Repayments must come from profit, not from capital.

20
00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:16.580
For example, Esther's drinks business makes about 120,000 naira net profit a month.

21
00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:22.100
She draws 80,000 for the household, so 40,000 is left.

22
00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:35.190
The loan repayment would be 55,000 naira a month. She would be short by 15,000 every month. So she asks for a smaller loan or a longer period before she signs anything.

23
00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:42.430
Never pay a processing fee to someone who calls or messages you offering a loan or grant.

24
00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:52.730
Never share your BVN, card PIN or one-time codes with anyone. And do not keep bank PINs or passwords in your spreadsheet.

25
00:03:53.980 --> 00:04:07.480
Before using a loan app, check it is on the approved list published by the Federal Competition and Consumer Protection Commission, the FCCPC. Read the total amount you will repay, not only the monthly figure.

26
00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:17.710
Keep your notebook as the original record, and let the phone do the adding. Install Google Sheets, sign in, and type five headings in row 1.

27
00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:29.250
Type your opening balance in cell E2. In E3, type equals E2 plus C3 minus D3, and copy it down. The spreadsheet adds for you.

28
00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:44.150
For example, Kemi types her bread totals each night, and her sheet showed 31,600 naira, matching her cash and app balance. Lock your phone, and photograph your paper pages every week as a backup.

29
00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:54.830
Now read one month of your records, calculate one month's profit, build your record pack, and build your phone cash book. Continue with the lessons below.
